#: src/std-traits/default.md:6
msgid "The `Default` Trait"
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:8
msgid ""
"The [`Default`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/default/trait.Default.html) "
"trait produces a default value for a type."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:11
msgid "// Copyright 2023 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:12
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:26
msgid "\"John Smith\""
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:35
msgid "\"Y is set!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:46
msgid ""
"It can be implemented directly or it can be derived via `#[derive(Default)]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:47
msgid ""
"A derived implementation will produce a value where all fields are set to "
"their default values."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:49
msgid "This means all types in the struct must implement `Default` too."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:50
msgid ""
"Standard Rust types commonly implement `Default` with reasonable values (e."
"g. `0`, `\"\"`, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:52
msgid "The partial struct initialization works nicely with default."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:53
msgid ""
"The Rust standard library is aware that types can implement `Default` and "
"provides convenience methods that use it."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-traits/default.md:55
msgid ""
"The `..` syntax is called [struct update syntax](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"book/ch05-01-defining-structs.html#creating-instances-from-other-instances-"
"with-struct-update-syntax)."
msgstr ""
